<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">orders</ansi>

A battle plays out on its own, by how you set it up beforehand. Orders are
the "when this happens, do that" part of the setup: up to <ansi fg="yellow">3 orders</ansi> for each
member of your company, you included. Each round, before a member's role and
target rule (<ansi fg="command">help strategy</ansi>), it reads its orders in the order you set them.
The first whose condition holds and that it can carry out fires, and takes
the member's turn. If none does, it fights by its strategy as before.

  <ansi fg="command">Brother Oswin tends you, as ordered.</ansi>

The battle log says "as ordered" whenever one fires. An order that keeps
holding (a guard, a hold) is noted when it starts, not every round.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Conditions</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">ally [n]</ansi>        an ally is below n% of its health (n is a multiple of 5,
                  from 10 to 90). It means the most hurt ally who is not the
                  member itself. A player who is down but still alive
                  counts (and is the most hurt); a dead one does not.
  <ansi fg="command">self [n]</ansi>        the member itself is below n% of its health.
  <ansi fg="command">chanting</ansi>        a foe is chanting a spell (<ansi fg="command">help interrupts</ansi>).
  <ansi fg="command">boss</ansi>            a boss stands among the foes.
  <ansi fg="command">first</ansi>           it is the battle's first round (against each group in
                  turn, where several wait).
  <ansi fg="command">foe [kind]</ansi>      a foe of that kind stands: <ansi fg="command">caster</ansi> or <ansi fg="command">healer</ansi>.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Actions</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">heal</ansi>        cast a heal at the ally (or at itself, for <ansi fg="command">self</ansi>) the
                  condition named: the heavy heal for one in trouble. Needs a
                  healing spell the member knows and can pay for. An ally a
                  heal already covers is passed over. Goes with <ansi fg="command">ally</ansi> or
                  <ansi fg="command">self</ansi>.
  <ansi fg="command">break</ansi>       turn on the foe the condition named: for <ansi fg="command">chanting</ansi>, the chanter,
                  to break its chant. A fighter strikes it if it can reach it;
                  a member that casts (or a fighter that cannot reach it)
                  sends an attack spell at it, whatever its mana reserve. A
                  healer or caster with no attack spell to send passes the
                  order over. With two chanters, it takes the first it can
                  turn on. Goes with <ansi fg="command">chanting</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">boss</ansi> or <ansi fg="command">foe</ansi>.
  <ansi fg="command">guard</ansi>       step in front of the ally the condition named for this
                  round, as a guardian would (<ansi fg="command">help guardian</ansi>): it spends the
                  member's guards, and is read again every round. With no
                  guard left, or the ally too far off to guard, the order is
                  passed over. Goes with <ansi fg="command">ally</ansi>.
  <ansi fg="command">strongest</ansi>   cast the strongest attack spell it knows and can pay for,
                  whatever its mana reserve says. Any condition.
  <ansi fg="command">hold</ansi>        cast no attack spell this round (Lightning included),
                  keeping its mana: heals, hexes, wards, summons and weather
                  go on as usual. A fighter casts nothing anyway, so a hold
                  means nothing to one. Any condition.

An order whose action the member cannot carry out (no healing spell, no
mana, nothing in reach) is passed over for the next. <ansi fg="command">orders</ansi> warns when a member
has no spell for an order you add.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Commands</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">orders</ansi>                              everyone's orders
  <ansi fg="command">orders [who]</ansi>                        one member's, and the menus
  <ansi fg="command">orders [who] add [condition] then [action]</ansi>
                                      e.g. <ansi fg="command">orders oswin add ally 50 then heal</ansi>
  <ansi fg="command">orders [who] remove [number]</ansi>        take one off
  <ansi fg="command">orders [who] up [number]</ansi>            read it before the one above
  <ansi fg="command">orders [who] preset</ansi>                 the starting set for its class
  <ansi fg="command">orders [who] clear</ansi>                  none

[who] is <ansi fg="command">me</ansi>, or a companion by name (part of it will do) or number (<ansi fg="command">#2</ansi>).

Order matters: put the one you care about most first. Set orders before a
battle: during one they can be read but not changed. They are kept when you
log out. <ansi fg="command">strategy [who] default</ansi> leaves them alone; <ansi fg="command">orders [who] clear</ansi> is how
they go.

In the web client, the Combat tab lists each member's orders under their
row, with an Orders button whose menu adds, removes and reorders them
(<ansi fg="command">help webclient</ansi>).

See also: <ansi fg="command">help stances</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help strategy</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help guardian</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help interrupts</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help tactics</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help combat</ansi>.
